    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> won independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1948 as a federal union of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Bamar</a> majority and many ethnic minorities.</li><li>The army has ruled for most of the period since 1962, first through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a>'s isolationist socialism and then through juntas.</li><li>Brief reforms after 2011 ended with the coup of 2021, which turned decades of borderland insurgency into nationwide war.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Burmese diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-thant">U Thant</a>, a close friend of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a>, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a> from 1961 to 1971, the first Asian in that post.</p></aside>
    </section>

